## Proof

Let $X = \{(t,0,0):t\in\mathbb{R}\}\cup\{(0,t,0):t\in\mathbb{R}\}\cup\{(0,0,t):t\in\mathbb{R}\}$ be the union of the three coordinate axes in $\mathbb{R}^3$.

### Step 1: Deformation retraction to $S^2$ minus 6 points

Since $X$ passes through the origin, $\mathbb{R}^3\setminus X \subset \mathbb{R}^3\setminus\{0\}$. Define the radial deformation
$$H(p,t) = (1-t)\,p + t\,\frac{p}{|p|}, \qquad p\in\mathbb{R}^3\setminus X,\; t\in[0,1].$$

Writing $H(p,t) = \bigl((1-t)|p|+t\bigr)\cdot\frac{p}{|p|}$, we see $H(p,t)$ lies on the open ray $\{s\cdot u : s>0\}$ where $u = p/|p|$ and $s=(1-t)|p|+t>0$. Since $p\notin X$, the direction $u$ is not parallel to any coordinate axis, so this ray is disjoint from $X$ (it meets a coordinate axis only at the origin, $s=0$, which is not attained). Hence $H(p,t)\in\mathbb{R}^3\setminus X$ for all $t$.

We have $H(p,0)=p$, $H(p,1)=p/|p|\in S^2$, and $H$ fixes every point of $S^2\setminus(X\cap S^2)$. Thus $H$ is a deformation retraction:
$$\mathbb{R}^3\setminus X \;\simeq\; S^2\setminus(X\cap S^2).$$

Now $X\cap S^2 = \{\pm e_1,\,\pm e_2,\,\pm e_3\}$, a set of **6 points**. So $\mathbb{R}^3\setminus X \simeq S^2\setminus\{6\text{ points}\}$.

### Step 2: $S^2$ minus 6 points is a bouquet of 5 circles

Remove 6 small disjoint open disks around the 6 punctures. The remaining compact surface $S^2$ minus 6 open disks is a sphere with 6 boundary components, which deformation retracts onto a 1-dimensional spine (a graph). Its Euler characteristic is
$$\chi = 2 - 6 = -4 = 1 - n \implies n = 5.$$
A connected graph with $\chi = 1-n$ is a bouquet of $n$ circles. Therefore
$$S^2\setminus\{6\text{ points}\} \;\simeq\; \bigvee_{5} S^1,$$
and
$$\pi_1(\mathbb{R}^3\setminus X)\cong\pi_1\!\bigl(S^2\setminus\{6\text{ points}\}\bigr)\cong F_5,$$
the **free group of rank 5**.

### Step 3: Explicit generators

Label the 6 punctures on $S^2$:
$$p_1=e_1,\; p_2=-e_1,\; p_3=e_2,\; p_4=-e_2,\; p_5=e_3,\; p_6=-e_3.$$

Choose the basepoint $b = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}}(1,1,1)\in S^2\setminus\{p_1,\dots,p_6\}$.

For each $i=1,\dots,5$, choose a path $\alpha_i$ on $S^2$ from $b$ to a point near $p_i$ (avoiding all punctures), and let
$$\gamma_i = \alpha_i\cdot\mu_i\cdot\alpha_i^{-1},$$
where $\mu_i$ is a small positively-oriented (counterclockwise as seen from outside $S^2$) loop encircling $p_i$ once.

Concretely, in $\mathbb{R}^3\setminus X$ these correspond to:

| Generator | Links the ray |
|-----------|--------------|
| $\gamma_1$ | positive $x$-ray $\{(t,0,0):t>0\}$ |
| $\gamma_2$ | negative $x$-ray $\{(t,0,0):t<0\}$ |
| $\gamma_3$ | positive $y$-ray $\{(0,t,0):t>0\}$ |
| $\gamma_4$ | negative $y$-ray $\{(0,t,0):t<0\}$ |
| $\gamma_5$ | positive $z$-ray $\{(0,0,t):t>0\}$ |

For instance, $\gamma_1$ is realized as: travel from $b$ along $S^2$ to the point $\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+\varepsilon^2}}(1,\varepsilon,0)$ near $p_1$, traverse the small circle $\bigl\{\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+\varepsilon^2}}(1,\,\varepsilon\cos\theta,\,\varepsilon\sin\theta) : \theta\in[0,2\pi]\bigr\}$ (which links the positive $x$-axis), and return to $b$ along the same path.

**The single relation.** The 6th meridian $\mu_6$ (around $p_6=-e_3$, the negative $z$-ray) is not independent. On $S^2$, the product of all 6 meridians is null-homotopic (a loop encircling all punctures can be slid over the sphere to the opposite side and contracted), giving:
$$\gamma_1\,\gamma_2\,\gamma_3\,\gamma_4\,\gamma_5\,\gamma_6 = 1 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \gamma_6 = (\gamma_1\gamma_2\gamma_3\gamma_4\gamma_5)^{-1}$$
(up to conjugation depending on the choice of connecting paths). This single relation reduces the rank from 6 to 5.

### Conclusion

The fundamental group of the complement of the three coordinate axes in $\mathbb{R}^3$ is the **free group of rank 5**:
$$\boxed{\pi_1\!\bigl(\mathbb{R}^3\setminus\{\text{three coordinate axes}\}\bigr)\cong F_5,}$$
freely generated by $\{\gamma_1,\gamma_2,\gamma_3,\gamma_4,\gamma_5\}$, the five meridian loops around five of the six half-axes (the sixth being determined by the sphere relation).

### PROOF COMPLETE
